      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Current trends in Social Capital Theory reveal a rich shift from purely structural networks to more nuanced analyses of embeddedness and relational dynamics. While **Putnam** established the focus on bridging vs. bonding capital, contemporary scholarship, informed by **Bourdieu**'s concept of capital conversion, examines how social resources translate into material advantage. Furthermore, work by **Granovetter** on weak ties continues to inform analyses of structural holes, often contrasted with **Burt**'s focus on brokerage and network positionality. These evolutions demonstrate a move beyond simple accumulation to understanding relational quality, drawing on insights from **Coleman** and **Portes** regarding context-specific resource activation.",
      "source": "target",
